{{#> layouts/base head-sub-title="SMTP connection pooling and batch orchestration" page-title="SMTP connection pooling and batch orchestration" }}

	{{#*inline "html-head-block"}}
<meta name="Description" content="Choose between sequential connection reuse, Simple Java Mail batch pooling, the standalone batch facade, direct leases, Jakarta Mail smtppool, and Camel.">
	{{/inline}}

	{{#*inline "header-block"}}
		{{> includes/header topic="modules" }}
	{{/inline}}

	{{#*inline "body-block"}}
  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      There is one underlying connection-pool implementation and several ways to orchestrate it. The right choice depends less on raw throughput than on
      which layer already owns your messages, work queue, Jakarta Mail <code class="inline language-java">Session</code>, and shutdown boundary.
    </p>

    <div class="pooling-question-flow" aria-label="Connection-pooling chooser">
      <article>
        <h2>Do you need a pool?</h2>
        <p>If one sequential callback can reuse one connection, choose <a href="#option-no-pool"><code>withOpenConnection</code> or simple batch</a>.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h2>Do you use <code>Mailer</code>?</h2>
        <p>Let <a href="#option-mailer">Simple Java Mail own callbacks, leases, the pool, and shutdown</a>.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h2>Who owns the work?</h2>
        <p>Choose the <a href="#option-batch-facade">standalone batch facade</a> for callbacks/futures, or the <a href="#option-direct-pool">direct pool</a> for explicit leases.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h2>Does a framework own Transport?</h2>
        <p>Use the <a href="#option-provider"><code>smtppool</code> provider</a> for Jakarta Mail/Spring, or the <a href="#option-camel">Camel adapter</a>.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <blockquote class="pool-owner-rule">
      <strong>Single physical-pool owner</strong>
      Exactly one component owns the physical connection pool. Never place <code>batch-module</code> or a direct pool around an
      <code>smtppool</code> transport.
    </blockquote>
  </section>

  <a href="#ownership" id="ownership" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Follow the ownership lane</h2>
  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      Each row is a complete lifecycle. Moving across rows mid-flow creates ambiguous release, failure, and shutdown behavior.
    </p>
